125→    <div class="at-a-glance">
126→      <div class="glance-title">At a Glance</div>
127→      <div class="glance-sections">
128→        <div class="glance-section"><strong>What's working:</strong> You have a sharp eye for when Claude over-engineers — catching unnecessary structs, reverting scope creep, and redirecting toward simpler solutions. Your performance debugging workflow is particularly strong, chaining stack traces with TDD to squash serious bottlenecks like the CLI hang you took from infinite to 0.8 seconds. You also get a lot of leverage from multi-agent code reviews on large branches, then immediately fixing what&apos;s found. <a href="#section-wins" class="see-more">Impressive Things You Did →</a></div>
129→        <div class="glance-section"><strong>What's hindering you:</strong> On Claude&apos;s side, it too often jumps into coding before fully understanding the problem — picking wrong approaches (like the Firefox porting attempts) or making unnecessary changes you have to revert. On your side, some friction comes from not scoping requests tightly enough upfront, especially on large branch reviews where Claude reviews the wrong diff or makes changes beyond what you asked for. <a href="#section-friction" class="see-more">Where Things Go Wrong →</a></div>
130→        <div class="glance-section"><strong>Quick wins to try:</strong> Try creating custom slash commands (/review, /perf-debug) that bake in your preferred constraints like &quot;analyze before coding&quot; and &quot;minimal scoped changes only&quot; — this would eliminate the repeated redirections you&apos;re doing manually. You could also set up hooks to auto-run linting and compilation after edits, catching those leftover unused variables and compile errors that keep requiring extra cleanup passes. <a href="#section-features" class="see-more">Features to Try →</a></div>
131→        <div class="glance-section"><strong>Ambitious workflows:</strong> As models get more capable, your review-then-fix workflow could become fully autonomous — Claude reviews a branch, fixes issues with tests, and presents a clean diff without the back-and-forth that currently causes scope creep. Your performance investigation work would also benefit hugely from parallel agents simultaneously profiling different subsystems, so you get a complete root cause analysis instead of discovering bottlenecks one at a time. <a href="#section-horizon" class="see-more">On the Horizon →</a></div>
