852→        <div class="horizon-card">
853→          <div class="horizon-title">Parallel Agent Performance Investigation Workflow</div>
854→          <div class="horizon-possible">Several of your highest-value sessions involved tracing performance bottlenecks across multiple subsystems (3.3s PostCommit, 200% CPU hang, 20s hook overhead). Instead of sequential investigation where Claude sometimes fixes only one of multiple bottlenecks, parallel agents could simultaneously profile different code paths and converge on a complete root cause analysis before any code changes.</div>
855→          <div class="horizon-tip"><strong>Getting started:</strong> Leverage TaskCreate/Agent tools to spawn parallel investigation agents, each with a focused scope, then synthesize findings before implementing any fix.</div>
856→          <div class="pattern-prompt"><div class="prompt-label">Paste into Claude Code:</div><code>I&apos;m seeing a performance issue: [describe symptom]. Before writing any code changes, investigate the root cause thoroughly. Spawn 3 parallel sub-agents: Agent 1 - trace the hot path from entry point and identify all operations taking &gt;100ms using profiling or timing instrumentation. Agent 2 - search the codebase for known expensive patterns (full file reads, unbounded loops, blocking calls, tree diffs) in the affected code paths. Agent 3 - review recent git history for changes that could have introduced the regression. Each agent should report findings independently. Then synthesize all findings into a ranked list of bottlenecks with estimated impact. Only after I approve the analysis, implement fixes one at a time with before/after benchmarks, committing each separately.</code><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyText(this)">Copy</button></div>
857→        </div>
858→      
859→        <div class="horizon-card">
860→          <div class="horizon-title">Test-Driven Merge Conflict Resolution at Scale</div>
861→          <div class="horizon-possible">You had a standout session where Claude resolved 51 merge conflicts and fixed cascading compilation errors—rated essential. This could become a repeatable autonomous workflow where Claude resolves conflicts, runs the compiler and full test suite in a loop, and handles the cascading breakage (like your context parameter threading issues) without human intervention.</div>
862→          <div class="horizon-tip"><strong>Getting started:</strong> Set up Claude Code with a Bash loop pattern: resolve conflicts, compile, fix errors, run tests, iterate until green—leveraging your Go and TypeScript test suites as the verification oracle.</div>
863→          <div class="pattern-prompt"><div class="prompt-label">Paste into Claude Code:</div><code>Merge main into the current branch. For any merge conflicts: 1) Analyze both sides of each conflict to understand the intent of each change. 2) Resolve favoring the semantics of our branch while incorporating main&apos;s structural changes. 3) After resolving all conflicts, run `go build ./...` and fix any compilation errors iteratively until clean. 4) Run `go test ./...` and fix any test failures, distinguishing between conflicts you introduced vs pre-existing failures. 5) If any fix requires understanding design intent you&apos;re unsure about, flag it for my review rather than guessing. 6) Commit the merge with a message summarizing the key conflict resolutions and any non-obvious decisions you made. Do not stop until compilation and tests pass or you&apos;ve hit something that genuinely requires my input.</code><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyText(this)">Copy</button></div>
864→        </div>
